LEGENDARY, AS SIX SKINS -- the measured output of
dev/legendary/BUILD_LEGENDARY_PAINT.luau, one run per framing.

Both runs calibrate the same six shapes to the same 45% coverage, so the two
tables differ only in the body size the cells were built at. The feature columns
are reported at BOTH framings from either run, which is the whole point of
having two of them.

RULE 3 CARRIES THREE NUMBERS NOW, and the third one is here because of a
failure the first two could not see. `rosette` -- lilac (185,140,235) with deep
violet (60,25,110) rings -- rendered as ONE COLOUR WITH TEXTURE and measured:

    lum gap 0.462   (2.9x the ladder's own worst, 0.161)
    rgb     0.827   (1.08x the ladder's own worst, 0.767)
    hue     4 deg

It passed both gates it had. It is the same violet at two brightnesses, and a
colour at two brightnesses is exactly what a LIGHT does to one colour -- the
terminator rule arriving in colour space. contrast() came from BUILD_SKINS,
where the accent is always white; white has no hue, so hue separation was never
a variable there and a constant is a thing nobody misses. Rule 1 is what put a
hue on both sides of the comparison, so rule 1 is what made the third number
necessary.

